WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.862
Code that waits. The same job, written three ways.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.633
Two requests reach your server. The first reads a file with `readFileSync`.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:16.996
That line waits for the disk. The second request waits behind it.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:23.776
Say eighty milliseconds each. With a hundred users, the last one waits eight seconds.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:30.452
Plain `readFile` starts the work and returns at once. The next line runs straight away.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.013
The data arrives later, in a callback.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:42.688
Here is the job. Read `config.json`. Read the data file it names. Write a copy.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.918
With callbacks, each step sits inside the one before. The code drifts to the right.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:56.846
Every callback receives the error first, then the result. So every level repeats: if error, return.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.040
Three steps, three levels, three error checks.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.998
A promise is an object standing for a result that is not ready yet.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:13.933
It starts pending. Then it settles once: fulfilled with a value, or rejected with an error.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:20.079
`.then` receives the value and returns a new promise. So the steps chain flat.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:24.401
One `.catch` at the end handles a failure in any step.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.849
`async` and `await` write the same chain as ordinary lines.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:35.529
`await` pauses this function until the promise settles, then hands back the value.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:41.078
It reads top to bottom. The function waits; the thread stays free.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:45.270
Same three steps. Only the spelling changed.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:52.608
Now the classic mistakes. Forget `await`, and you hold a promise, not the task.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:59.151
`task.title` is `undefined`. Log the task, and you see `Promise { <pending> }`.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:06.161
Sent as JSON, a promise becomes an empty object. Missing await: look there first.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:13.038
A rejected promise makes `await` throw on that line. So `try` and `catch` works.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:17.752
Without it, an unhandled rejection ends the Node process.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:26.224
Every user is disconnected, not just the one whose request failed. With the catch, that one user gets a `500`.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:32.813
And the `await` must sit inside the `try`. Otherwise the error arrives after the net is gone.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:43.108
Last: two independent queries. `findUser` takes 100 milliseconds. `findTasks` takes 120.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:49.468
`await` one, then the other, and the waits stack. 220 milliseconds.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:56.978
Calling the function starts the work. Await only waits. So start both, then wait once.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:03.599
`Promise.all` takes about the slowest: 120. The results come back in order.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:09.122
Use it when the work is independent. One rejection rejects them all.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:15.307
Practise it with the event-loop visualiser in Server-side JavaScript.
